---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import FamilyLine from '../components/FamilyLine.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import CardDetail from '../components/CardDetail.astro';
import { product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('edge-relay');
const latest = (await getCollection('releases'))
  .filter((r) => r.data.product === 'core' && r.data.published)
  .sort((a, b) => b.data.version.localeCompare(a.data.version, undefined, { numeric: true }))[0];
const v = latest.data.version;
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop Edge Relay: a Sparkplug B edge node"
  description="HotLoop Edge Relay polls equipment and forwards it over Sparkplug B, with no database and no UI, and buffers to disk when the uplink is down."
  ogAlt="HotLoop Edge Relay, a headless Sparkplug B edge node."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/edge-relay/',
      description:
        'A headless poll-and-forward Sparkplug B edge node with no database, no UI and no write path, using the same protocol drivers as HotLoop Gateway.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      softwareVersion: v,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop Edge Relay</span><span class="chip ok">{v} is out</span></div>
      <h1>Poll, forward, survive. Nothing else.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A headless edge node for a Pi or a small industrial PC. It polls equipment with the same drivers the Gateway
        uses, forwards every reading over Sparkplug B, and buffers to disk when the uplink goes away. No database, no
        UI, no login, and no way to write to a machine. That's the feature: a box with nothing to click can't be
        clicked wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>Edge Relay {v} is out, released in lockstep with the Gateway.</strong>
          <p>
            The image is <code>{p.image}:{v}</code> for amd64, arm64 and arm/v7, about 15 MB, and it pulls with no login. The
            chart is <code>{p.chart}</code> from <code>https://hotloop.io/hotloop</code>, and there is a Quadlet unit
            for a box that isn't in a cluster.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install it</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
        <a class="btn" href="https://docs.hotloop.io/edge-relay/overview/">Docs</a>
      </div>
      <FamilyLine id="edge-relay" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What it is not</span>
        <h2>Not a smaller Gateway with the options turned off.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          It's a different binary. It never links the database, the web UI, the automation engine, the entity
          registry or MCP, and a test asks the compiler what actually got linked and fails the build if any of them
          sneaks in. There's no "relay mode" buried in the Gateway, because a mode is one bad environment variable
          away from a web server listening on a box nobody's watching.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Every driver</h3><p>OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT and Sparkplug B, EtherNet/IP, S7comm, MTConnect, HTTP, and UniFi, from the same code the Gateway polls with.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Store-and-forward</h3><p>Sparkplug B births, sequence numbers and deaths. When the link drops it queues to one file on disk and replays in order, flagged historical.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Configured by a file</h3><p>Devices and tags come from one JSON file. A bad file is refused and the old one keeps running. A pushed file applies the moment it lands.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Finds devices that announce themselves</h3><p>The relay listens to MQTT brokers for ESPHome, Zigbee2MQTT, Tasmota, and Shelly devices, read and forward only. It still can't write to a thing.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>No write path</h3><p>It reads and forwards. It can't take a write, serve a screen or answer an agent, because it has no users to pin an action on.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Managed from the Gateway</h3><p>The Gateway's fleet API tracks every relay from its births and deaths, and sends a rebirth, a restart, or a new device file over the connection the relay dialed out on.</p></article>
        <article class="card reveal"><h3>Honest about what's proven</h3><p>Run end to end against a real Mosquitto broker, Modbus TCP slaves, an HTTP endpoint, and an OPC UA test server. Not yet run on real EtherNet/IP or S7comm hardware.</p></article>
      </div>
      <CardDetail>
        <div><h4>Every driver</h4><p>OPC UA, Modbus, MQTT and Sparkplug B, EtherNet/IP, S7comm, MTConnect, HTTP, and UniFi. The same code the Gateway polls with, down to the reconnect and back-off, so a bug fixed there is fixed here.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Store-and-forward</h4><p>Births, sequence numbers and deaths the way Sparkplug B says. When the link drops it queues to one file on disk and replays in order, flagged historical, when it's back. The historian upstream gets the gap filled instead of a hole.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Configured by a file</h4><p>Devices and tags come from one JSON file, and a file the Gateway pushes is read at start. A bad file is refused and the old one keeps running. A pushed file also applies the moment it lands: new devices connect, removed ones let go, changed ones reconnect, and the ones nobody touched keep their sessions. The Sparkplug session stays up with no death certificate, and a removed metric goes out once as null so the Gateway stops showing its last good value forever.</p></div>
        <div><h4>No write path</h4><p>It reads and forwards. It can't take a write, serve a screen or answer an agent, because every one of those needs a signed-in person to pin the action on, and it has no users at all.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Managed from the Gateway</h4><p>The Gateway's fleet API (no screen yet) tracks every relay you register from its births and deaths, and sends a rebirth, a restart, or a new device file over the connection the relay already dialed out on. Every birth also carries the relay's version and image digest, so the fleet list shows what each one runs, and an admin can approve an update a relay is holding. A pushed file lands on the relay's volume and wins over the chart's devices from 4.17.0 on; a 4.16.0 relay dropped it on the floor. Commands need no way in to the edge box, and nobody drives out to it.</p></div>
        <div><h4>Honest about what's proven</h4><p>Run end to end against a real Mosquitto broker, polling an HTTP endpoint, Modbus TCP slaves, and an OPC UA test server, with live config reload proven the same way. Discovery is proven against recordings of real devices, not a bench of them. It hasn't been run against real EtherNet/IP or S7comm hardware or a certified Sparkplug host yet. Until it has, this card stays here.</p></div>
      </CardDetail>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="install">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">Install</span>
        <h2>Helm on a cluster, Quadlet on a box.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Two supported ways, and both are containers that start on boot and come back when they die. A bare
          {' '}<code>podman run</code> from an SSH session is not one of them, because it dies the moment you close
          the laptop, and you'll find out from the historian a week later.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid grid-2 split">
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Helm</h3>
          <p class="muted">The broker and the Sparkplug group are required, and the chart refuses to render without them.</p>
<pre><code>helm repo add hotloop https://hotloop.io/hotloop
helm repo update
helm install press-line-3 hotloop/hotloop-edge-relay \
  --version {v} --namespace hotloop --create-namespace \
  --set edgePublish.broker=tcp://gateway.plant.local:1883 \
  --set edgePublish.group=Plant1 \
  -f devices.yaml</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            Devices and tags go in <code>devices.yaml</code> under <code>devices:</code> and <code>tags:</code>. If a
            device carries a password, put the whole device file in a Secret and name it in
            {' '}<code>devicesSecret</code>, because values end up in a ConfigMap anyone in the namespace can read.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="stack">
          <h3>Quadlet</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            For a Pi or an industrial PC outside any cluster. Quadlet turns the container into a systemd service:
            it starts on boot with nobody logged in, restarts when it dies, and logs to the journal. It needs Podman
            5.0 or newer, so Raspberry Pi OS Bookworm, which ships 4.3, is too old for it. The unit follows
            {' '}<code>:stable</code> and rolls a bad update back.
          </p>
          <p class="muted">
            Save <code>edge-relay.container</code> and <code>edge-relay-data.volume</code> from the
            {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/edge-relay/install/">install docs</a> into
            {' '}<code>/etc/containers/systemd/</code>, set the broker, group, and node in the first, and put your
            device file at <code>/etc/edge-relay/devices.json</code>. Then:
          </p>
<pre><code>printf '%s' 'your-broker-password' | \
  sudo podman secret create edge-relay-broker-password -
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start edge-relay.service
curl localhost:8081/status</code></pre>
          <p class="muted">
            Broker takes anonymous connections? Skip the secret and delete the unit's <code>Secret=</code> line. On an
            isolated OT network that's a legit setup, not a mistake.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            The Edge Relay is under the HotLoop Business Source License 1.1, like the rest of the lineup. Home and
            lab use is free. Business use is free through <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNet</a>.
            Support, SLAs, and warranties come from our Official Systems Integrator,
            {' '}<a href="https://fireballz.ai">Fireball Industries</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the license</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .page-hero .notice code { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .split h3 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-lg); }
  pre { max-width: 100%; }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
